=== L1 Components of Health and Fitness ===
id: eb82a951-7e76-4a3e-88fc-4971b8879c0e
slug: lesson-01
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:55:35.959878+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 37

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Definitions and sporting examples for all fitness components, distinguishing health-related from skill-related groups.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Health and Fitness?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Before looking at individual components, it helps to understand what we mean by the two terms. <dfn class="term" data-def="A state of complete physical, mental and social wellbeing, not merely the absence of disease or injury.">Health</dfn> is a broad concept — it refers to complete physical, mental and social wellbeing, not just the absence of illness. <dfn class="term" data-def="The ability to meet the physical demands of a specific sport or physical activity effectively and without undue fatigue.">Fitness</dfn>, by contrast, is the ability to meet the demands of a specific sport or task. A person can be healthy without being particularly fit, and a very fit athlete can still have health concerns. In sport, coaches and sport scientists care about both — but fitness components are what they measure and train.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Health-Related vs Skill-Related Components</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The components of fitness are divided into two groups. <dfn class="term" data-def="Components of fitness that are most closely linked to overall physical health and general wellbeing, including aerobic endurance, muscular endurance, muscular strength, flexibility, and body composition.">Health-related components</dfn> underpin general physical wellbeing and are relevant to everyone, whether or not they play sport: aerobic endurance (cardiovascular endurance), muscular endurance, muscular strength, flexibility, and body composition. <dfn class="term" data-def="Components of fitness that are most strongly linked to athletic performance and the execution of sport-specific skills, including speed, power, agility, balance, coordination, and reaction time.">Skill-related components</dfn> are performance-oriented — they are most important for sport and physical activity: speed, power, agility, balance, coordination, and reaction time. In the written exam you may need to classify a component into the correct group, so knowing both lists is essential.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall the five health-related and six skill-related components from memory. This classification is commonly tested.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Health-related components (5): aerobic endurance, muscular endurance, muscular strength, flexibility, body composition. Skill-related components (6): speed, power, agility, balance, coordination, reaction time.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Health-Related Components in Detail</h2>
<h3 data-narration-id="n7">Aerobic Endurance (Cardiovascular Endurance)</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Aerobic endurance is the ability of the heart, lungs and circulatory system to supply oxygen to working muscles over a prolonged period of exercise. It is sometimes called cardiovascular endurance. It is essential for activities that last a long time at a moderate-to-high intensity — marathon running, long-distance cycling and rowing all demand high aerobic endurance. A footballer also needs aerobic endurance to maintain their performance level across a full 90-minute match.</p>

<h3 data-narration-id="n9">Muscular Endurance</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Muscular endurance is the ability of a muscle or muscle group to perform repeated contractions over a sustained period without becoming fatigued. It differs from muscular strength, which is about maximum force in a single effort. Sports that require muscular endurance include distance swimming, road cycling and cross-country running, where the same muscles must contract hundreds or thousands of times. A rower performing a 2,000-metre race needs high muscular endurance in the back, core and arm muscles to maintain stroke power throughout.</p>

<h3 data-narration-id="n11">Muscular Strength</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Muscular strength is the maximum force a muscle or muscle group can exert in a single contraction. It is important in sports that involve lifting, throwing, sprinting or explosive contact. A shot-putter needs very high muscular strength to generate force through the put. A weightlifter, a rugby forward clearing out a ruck or a gymnast holding a static position all depend heavily on muscular strength.</p>

<h3 data-narration-id="n13">Flexibility</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Flexibility is the range of movement possible at a joint. It is determined by the elasticity of the muscles, tendons and ligaments surrounding the joint. High flexibility reduces injury risk and can improve performance in sports where large ranges of movement are needed. Gymnastics and dance demand exceptional flexibility — a gymnast performing a full split or a diver entering a pike position both require high flexibility. Even team sport players benefit from good flexibility to avoid muscle strains and to move efficiently.</p>

<h3 data-narration-id="n15">Body Composition</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Body composition refers to the ratio of fat mass to lean mass (muscle, bone, organs and fluid) in the body. It is a health-related component because a high proportion of body fat is associated with increased risk of cardiovascular disease and reduced health, while a favourable body composition supports physical performance. Optimal body composition differs by sport — a heavyweight powerlifter may have a higher body mass with large muscle mass, while a distance runner is typically lean with a low body fat percentage to reduce the mass that must be carried over distance.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Skill-Related Components in Detail</h2>
<h3 data-narration-id="n18">Speed</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Speed is the ability to move the whole body, or a body part, from one point to another in the shortest possible time. A sprinter needs exceptional whole-body speed over 100 metres. A cricket or baseball batter needs high arm speed to swing the bat effectively, while a defender in football must have speed to track runs from attackers. Speed is most obviously tested by sprint tests such as a 30-metre sprint.</p>

<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Power</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n21"><dfn class="term" data-def="The ability to combine muscular strength and speed in a single explosive movement; sometimes expressed as the rate at which force is applied.">Power</dfn> is a combination of strength and speed — it is the ability to apply maximum force at speed in a single explosive movement. A high jumper needs power in their take-off leg to propel them upwards; a tennis player needs power in their shoulder and arm to drive a serve; a sprinter's start reaction out of the blocks is powered by a powerful leg drive. Power is measured by the vertical jump test or the standing broad jump.</p>

<h3 data-narration-id="n22">Agility</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n23">Agility is the ability to change body position or direction quickly and precisely while maintaining control and balance. It combines speed, coordination and balance, making it one of the most sport-specific attributes tested. A basketball player dribbling past defenders, a netball player evading a marker or a rugby player stepping around a tackle all demonstrate high agility. The Illinois agility test is the standard assessment used to measure it.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n24" data-revision-tip="Remember: agility = speed + direction change + control. Do not confuse agility with speed alone. The Illinois agility test assesses both turning and accelerating.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Agility is the ability to change direction quickly and with control — it is a skill-related component because it depends on coordination and balance as well as speed. It is most important in sports that involve frequent changes of direction, such as basketball, netball and rugby.</p>
</div>

<h3 data-narration-id="n25">Balance</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n26">Balance is the ability to maintain the centre of mass over the base of support, either while stationary (static balance) or while moving (dynamic balance). A gymnast holding a handstand or a figure skater landing a jump must have excellent static and dynamic balance. A surfer maintaining their position on a board demonstrates dynamic balance. Balance is commonly assessed using the stork stand test, which measures the ability to balance on one leg for as long as possible.</p>

<h3 data-narration-id="n27">Coordination</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n28">Coordination is the ability to use two or more body parts smoothly and efficiently at the same time in a controlled movement. Hand-eye coordination is crucial in racket sports, cricket batting and goalkeeping, where the hands must track and respond to a moving ball. Foot-eye coordination is essential for football and rugby. Coordination is measured by the alternate hand throw test, in which the performer throws and catches a ball alternately with each hand.</p>

<h3 data-narration-id="n29">Reaction Time</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n30">Reaction time is the time taken from the moment a stimulus is detected (such as a starting gun, an opponent's movement or a ball being released) to the start of the response. A sprinter must react instantly to the starting pistol; a goalkeeper must react to a shot from close range; a badminton player must react to a fast smash. Faster reaction time can make the difference between reaching a ball or missing it. It is assessed using the ruler drop test.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Which components matter most for team sports?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n31">Most team sports require a blend of several components. In football, aerobic endurance (90-minute match demands), speed (chasing the ball or outrunning defenders), agility (changing direction in tight spaces), coordination (controlling the ball) and reaction time (responding to a through ball) are all highly relevant. Muscular strength and power matter for heading, shooting and winning physical battles. The balance of components differs by position — a goalkeeper prioritises reaction time and power; a central midfielder prioritises aerobic endurance; a striker prioritises speed and agility. In the exam you may be asked to select and justify the most important component for a given position or sport, so always link your choice back to the demands of the role.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why do different sports prioritise different components?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n32">The demands of a sport determine which fitness components are most critical. A 100-metre sprinter needs maximal speed and power but relies far less on aerobic endurance because the race lasts under 11 seconds — the ATP-PC and anaerobic energy systems dominate. A distance swimmer needs high aerobic endurance and muscular endurance but does not need exceptional agility or reaction time. A gymnast requires elite flexibility, balance and coordination but is less dependent on cardiovascular endurance. Understanding this link between sport demands and fitness requirements is central to planning effective training programmes — a coach would waste time training a shot-putter for aerobic endurance when their event demands strength and power.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n34">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n35">Health-related components (aerobic endurance, muscular endurance, muscular strength, flexibility, body composition) underpin general physical wellbeing and are relevant to all activity levels.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n36">Skill-related components (speed, power, agility, balance, coordination, reaction time) are most strongly linked to athletic performance and sport-specific skills.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n37">Different sports prioritise different components depending on their physical demands — always link the component to a specific sporting example when answering exam questions.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n33">In the written exam you may be given a sporting scenario and asked to identify and justify the most important component of fitness for the performer. Always: (1) name the component precisely using the correct term (e.g. "aerobic endurance" rather than just "stamina"); (2) give a clear, specific definition; (3) link it directly to the demands of the sport — what does the performer actually have to do that requires this component? Avoid listing components without justification. If asked whether a component is health-related or skill-related, use the two classification groups and do not guess — memorise both lists.</p>

