 },
 {
  "text": "A basketball coach is planning a new six-week training programme for her squad. Evaluate how fitness testing before, during and after the programme can benefit both the coach and her players.\n\nIn your answer, refer to at least three reasons for fitness testing.",
  "type": "8 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (7-8 marks): At least three reasons from the specification are discussed with developed evaluation. Example points: baseline testing identifies individual strengths and weaknesses before the programme begins, allowing the coach to personalise the training plan rather than using a one-size-fits-all approach; mid-programme re-testing allows the coach to adapt the programme if progress is slower than expected; comparing results to normative data shows how players compare to peers and helps with team selection for competitive fixtures; SMART targets set from baseline data motivate players by giving them specific, measurable goals to aim at; post-programme testing shows whether the six-week block produced the intended adaptations. A well-developed answer evaluates the relative importance of these reasons (e.g. arguing that the motivational benefit is only effective if tests are reliable and if SMART targets are realistic).\nSecure (5-6 marks): At least two reasons discussed with some evaluation and reference to the scenario.\nDeveloping (3-4 marks): Two or more reasons stated but explanation is limited and evaluation is absent.\nEmerging (1-2 marks): Basic points, one reason, no evaluation. [8 marks]"
 }
